@startuml
!include https://raw.githubusercontent.com/proveo-ca/identity/main/proveo.puml
skinparam noteTextAlignment left
' purpose: the domain FORM the sbx Kit enforces, and the Squid form proveo used to emit into it
' covers: internal/sbx/domain.go
' covers: internal/sbx/domain_test.go
' covers: internal/backend/sandbox/sandbox.go
' covers: internal/backend/sandbox/sandbox_test.go
' The Kit schema this serves: sbx-kit-contract.puml (same directory).
' Why an allow-all baseline hid it: policy-baseline.puml (same directory).
' The plan whose forcing example this corrects: _spec/_plans/retire-docker-egress.puml
' Notes here are capped at 5 lines x 50 columns; one idea per anchor.
'
' ── AUTHORITATIVE SOURCES ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
'   kit reference : https://docs.docker.com/ai/sandboxes/customize/kit-reference/
'   policy allow  : https://docs.docker.com/reference/cli/sbx/policy/allow/network/
'   kit spec v2   : https://github.com/docker/sbx-kits-contrib/blob/main/spec/SPEC-v2.md
'
' ── THE ENFORCED PATTERN SET, QUOTED RATHER THAN PARAPHRASED ────────────────
' The kit reference lists seven pattern shapes and marks only THREE enforced:
'     api.example.com        exact host                    ENFORCED
'     api.example.com:8080   exact host and port           ENFORCED
'     *.example.com          single-label wildcard         ENFORCED
'     **.example.com         multi-label wildcard          parsed; pending
'     api.example.com:80-443 port range                    parsed; pending
'     api.example.com:*      port wildcard                 parsed; pending
'     10.0.0.0/8             CIDR                          parsed; pending
' And the CLI reference warns, in its own words: "example.com and *.example.com
' don't cover each other. You must specify both if you need to match the root
' domain and its subdomains."
'
' A LEADING DOT IS NOT ON THAT LIST. `.example.com` is Squid `dstdomain`
' notation, where it means "this domain and every subdomain". sbx has no such
' shape, so the pattern matches nothing at all — not the apex, not a subdomain.
'
' ── WHAT proveo EMITTED, AND WHY NOBODY SAW IT ──────────────────────────────
' `provider.Entry` carries TWO domain fields that look interchangeable and are
' not. `ACL` is a Squid body (`dstdomain .x.ai`); `Hosts` is the list the broker
' strips and injects on. Both were authored in Squid's vocabulary, because Squid
' was the only consumer when they were written.
'
' `Hosts` later acquired a SECOND consumer with a different grammar: the sbx Kit.
' `ResolveWith` returns `e.Hosts` verbatim, and sandbox.Spec copied that list
' into `permissions.network.allow` and into `credentials[].apiKey.inject[]
' .domain` with no translation. So a run emitted:
'     allow: [".cursor.com", ".cursor.sh"]
' which is three valid YAML strings and zero enforceable patterns.
'
' IT WAS INVISIBLE FOR TWO INDEPENDENT REASONS, and both are worth keeping:
'   REACH NEVER FAILED. On an allow-all host baseline a Kit ADDS allow rules and
'     cannot subtract (policy-baseline.puml), so a malformed allowlist costs
'     nothing an operator would notice. e2e/sbx_egress_test.go SKIPS its
'     confinement assertion on such a host for exactly this reason.
'   THE PROBE THAT LOOKED AT IT ASKED THE WRONG QUESTION. sbx-kit-capabilities
'     .puml records "The SUFFIX-PATTERN domain is accepted" — measured with
'     `sbx run credprobe-own`, which proves the kit PARSES. Parsing is not
'     matching. A pattern that matches nothing still parses.
'
' ── WHAT ACTUALLY BREAKS, AND IT IS NOT REACH ───────────────────────────────
' SPEC-v2 on `inject[].domain`: "Domain to inject the credential into. Must also
' be allowed in permissions.network." The domain matches no request, so the
' proxy never injects, and the request leaves as an unauthenticated tunnel.
'
' MEASURED inside a live sandbox on an allow-all baseline — sbx MITMs ONLY the
' hosts it holds a credential for, and the CONNECT reply says which is which:
'     github.com       HTTP/1.0 200 OK              ALPN unnegotiated  -> MITM
'     api.x.ai         200 Connection established   ALPN h2            -> tunnel
'     api2.cursor.sh   200 Connection established   ALPN h2            -> tunnel
' A tunnel carrying the ORIGIN certificate is injection that never happened.
' The agent then reports the PROVIDER's refusal, which reads to an operator as
' an egress denial. That misreading is the whole defect.
'
' ── THE RULE THIS FILE FIXES ────────────────────────────────────────────────
' Translate at the Kit boundary, and ONLY there. `Entry.ACL` keeps Squid's
' vocabulary because Squid still consumes it on the docker backend; `Entry.Hosts`
' keeps its stored form because the broker's strip/inject matching is its own.
' `sbx.AllowPatterns` is the one place the two grammars meet.
'
'   ".x.ai"          -> "x.ai", "*.x.ai"    both, because neither covers the other
'   "api2.cursor.sh" -> "api2.cursor.sh"    a bare host means exactly that host
'   "openrouter.ai"  -> "openrouter.ai"     never widened: the registry already
'                                           lists the wildcard half separately
'   "*.example.com"  -> "*.example.com"     already sbx's grammar; passed through
'   "**"             -> "**"                allow-all is a pattern, not a host
'   ".x.ai:443"      -> "x.ai:443", "*.x.ai:443"   the port rides both halves
'
' NOT WIDENING A BARE HOST is the load-bearing half. Squid's leading dot is an
' explicit statement that subdomains are included; a bare host is an explicit
' statement that they are not. Adding `*.foo` to every `foo` would grant reach
' the registry never asked for, and would do it silently.
'
' ── WHAT IS MEASURED HERE AND WHAT IS NOT ───────────────────────────────────
' MEASURED: the enforced pattern table (quoted above), the MITM/tunnel split
' (curl through the sandbox proxy), and that the emitted allowlist carried the
' leading-dot form (cmd/proveo/testdata/plan/sbx-cursor-forward.golden).
'
' NOT MEASURED, and deliberately named rather than assumed: whether `*.x.ai` is
' accepted in `inject[].domain` specifically. The reference documents ONE domain
' vocabulary and requires inject domains to also appear in permissions.network,
' so the same grammar is the only coherent reading — but the probe that would
' settle it needs a live sbx and a dummy key, the way the credprobe did. Until
' then this file claims the allowlist half only.
title sbx Kit domain form — where Squid's grammar stops and sbx's begins

package "internal/provider" as PROV <<host>> {
  component "Entry.ACL\n""dstdomain .x.ai""" as ACL <<app>>
  component "Entry.Hosts\n"".x.ai""" as HOSTS <<app>>
}

component "sbx.AllowPatterns\nthe one translation point" as XLATE <<app>>

package "the two consumers" as CONS <<host>> {
  component "Squid\ndocker backend only" as SQUID <<app>>
  component "sbx Kit\npermissions.network.allow\ncredentials[].inject[].domain" as KIT <<app>>
}

cloud "sbx proxy\n""gateway.docker.internal:3128""" as PROXY <<cloud>>

component "**the defect**\n"".x.ai"" matches nothing" as BUG <<error>>

ACL ARROW_MAIN SQUID : unchanged — Squid's own grammar
HOSTS ARROW_MAIN XLATE
XLATE ARROW_MAIN KIT : ""x.ai"" + ""*.x.ai""
KIT ARROW_MAIN PROXY
HOSTS ARROW_ERROR BUG : before: copied verbatim
BUG ARROW_ERROR PROXY : credential never injected

note bottom of BUG
  **Reach never failed, so nobody looked.**
  On an allow-all baseline a Kit adds and
  cannot subtract. A malformed allowlist
  costs nothing visible — until a credential
  has to match one of its entries.
end note

note top of XLATE
  **Both halves, or neither.**
  ""example.com"" and ""*.example.com"" do not
  cover each other — the CLI reference says so
  outright. A leading dot meant both in Squid,
  so it must become both here.
end note

note bottom of XLATE
  **A bare host is never widened.**
  Squid's dot SAYS subdomains are included;
  its absence says they are not. Adding
  ""*.foo"" to every ""foo"" would grant reach
  the registry never asked for.
end note

note right of KIT
  **SPEC-v2 ties the two fields together.**
  ""inject[].domain"" must ALSO appear in
  ""permissions.network"". So a pattern that
  matches nothing does not merely fail to
  allow — it fails to authenticate.
end note

note bottom of PROXY
  **The CONNECT reply names the mode.**
  ""200 OK"" with ALPN unnegotiated is a
  terminated (injected) leg; ""200 Connection
  established"" with ""h2"" is a plain tunnel
  carrying the origin certificate.
end note

legend bottom
  **What this file settles**
  ==
  The sbx Kit enforces exact host, host:port and ""*.label"" — nothing else. Squid's
  leading-dot ""dstdomain"" form is not a pattern sbx knows, and it reached the Kit
  because ""Entry.Hosts"" grew a second consumer without growing a second form.
  --
  **The translation lives at the boundary**, not in the registry: ""Entry.ACL"" still
  speaks Squid for the docker backend, and ""sbx.AllowPatterns"" is the only place the
  two grammars meet.
  ==
  **Still open**: whether ""*.x.ai"" is accepted in ""inject[].domain"" specifically.
  Named here rather than assumed, because the probe that proved suffix domains PARSE
  never asked whether they MATCH.
end legend
@enduml
